add('AGL2F2','targeted proof check','There are (4−1)(4−2)=6 invertible linear maps on F2^2 and four translations. The resulting 24 affine maps act faithfully on the four vectors, so the image is all S4. This also follows from the explicit enumeration in scan014_finite_certificate.json.')
add('PGL2F3','targeted proof check','GL2(F3) has (9−1)(9−3)=48 matrices and its scalar subgroup has order two, giving PGL2(F3) order 24. Its action on the four projective points is faithful: a projectivity fixing the two coordinate lines is represented by a diagonal matrix, and fixing their sum line forces equal diagonal entries. Hence this action identifies PGL2(F3) with S4, conditionally reading GP(1,F3) as PGL2(F3).')
add('AGL1F4','targeted proof check','Construct F4=F2[t]/(t^2+t+1). An affine map x↦ax+b has three nonzero multipliers and four translations, giving twelve distinct permutations. Every nonzero translation is a product of two disjoint transpositions and therefore even; multiplication by t or t+1 fixes zero and cycles the three nonzero elements, also even. Thus AGL1(F4) is precisely A4. The manuscript question mark, if visually confirmed, must remain despite this successful check.')
add('PGL2F4','targeted proof check','PGL2(F4) has order 4(4^2−1)=60 and acts faithfully on its five projective points by the same three-line argument. The explicit field/matrix enumeration checks that all sixty resulting permutations are even and exhaust A5. This verifies the displayed exceptional comparison under the GP=PGL interpretation.')
add('PGL2F5','computation or formal certificate','The script enumerates all 120 scalar classes of GL2(F5). The determinant-square subgroup has sixty classes and fifteen involutions. Their commuting triples form exactly five Klein-four subgroups, explicitly stored in the certificate. Conjugation by all 120 projectivities permutes these five subgroups and gives 120 distinct permutations; all 14,400 products satisfy the action law. Therefore the conjugation action is an isomorphism PGL2(F5)→S5. The written S5/GP(1,F5) identity is mathematically valid, rather than a slip; this conclusion does not select any unread glyph.')
add('EXACT','targeted proof check','For a nonzero vector space V over a field k, scalar multiplication embeds k* into GL(V). Defining PGL(V)=GL(V)/k* gives the exact sequence 1→k*→GL(V)→PGL(V)→1. For dim V=n≥1, this is the second sequence with projective index n−1. Nonzero dimension is required for the scalar map to be faithful in this form.')
add('NONSPLIT','targeted proof check','The central sequence need not split. Over F3, A=[[0,−1],[1,0]] has A^2=−I; its nonidentity projective class has order two, while its only two scalar lifts A and −A both have order four. A group section would have to lift that element to one whose square is identity, impossible. The finite script verifies the matrix squares. This establishes the literal in-general nonsplitting assertion; it does not assert that every such sequence is nonsplit.')
add('PROJ-NONZERO','targeted proof check','The one-dimensional subspaces of a nonzero vector space are the k* orbits on V without zero: every line is generated by any of its nonzero vectors, and two such vectors generate the same line exactly when they are scalar multiples. Thus P(V)=(V minus {0})/k*. GL(V) preserves these orbits, and its scalar kernel acts trivially, so PGL(V) acts on P(V).')
add('PROJ-ALLV','refuted','If the marginal V/k* is interpreted as the orbit set of the entire vector space, the asserted identification with P(V) is false: zero is one extra orbit. For V=F2^2 there are four such orbits but only three projective points. Refined statement id M014-PROJ-NONZERO deletes zero before quotienting. The possible shorthand is recorded conditionally; no zero or deletion sign is supplied to the transcription.')
add('VECTOR-STRUCT','independent re-proof','Let W=k^n and X be a fixed set. Each bijection f:W→X transports addition and scalar multiplication to a vector-space structure on X. Two bijections f and h transport the same operations exactly when f^−1 h belongs to GL(n,k). Therefore the right GL(n,k) orbits on Isom_ens(k^n,X) classify dimension-n k-vector structures on X. If no bijection exists, both the transport set and the possible structure set are empty.')
add('AFF-STRUCT','independent re-proof','The same transport argument for the standard affine space k^n shows that dimension-n affine k-structures on a fixed set E are Isom_ens(k^n,E)/Aff(n,k): two coordinate bijections transport the same affine structure exactly when their transition is affine. The group action is precomposition on the right. This verifies the proposed E-target correction in sweep SA01-K75-02 mathematically, pending visual adjudication of the written letter.')
add('AFF-MIXED','refuted','The formula with a structure on E but Isom_ens(k^n,V) on the right is not valid for unrelated E and V. Take k=F2,n=2, V=F2^2 and |E|=2, so V even has the vector structure discussed in the preceding example. The written right side has one orbit because its 24 bijections are acted on by Aff(2,F2)=S4. But E cannot carry an affine structure of dimension two, whose underlying set must have four points. Refined statement id M014-AFF-STRUCT uses E on both sides. The manuscript V, if confirmed, must be retained as a written mismatch.')
add('REGULAR-TORSOR','targeted proof check','A right G-torsor E, assumed nonempty, determines a regular subgroup of Sym(E) through its right translations. The assignment g↦R_g is an anti-homomorphism for usual composition, since R_g R_h=R_hg; its image Gamma is a group. Conversely a regular subgroup Gamma acts as a right torsor through Gamma^op by x·gamma=gamma(x). This gives the intrinsic unspecified-group description with the right/left convention stated, rather than silently identifying the right acting group with Gamma under the wrong multiplication.')
add('FAITHFUL-ACTION','targeted proof check','For a faithful action on a fixed set, the acting group is identified with its image subgroup of Sym(E), with the opposite convention for a right action if required. Conversely each such subgroup gives a faithful action. If faithfulness is dropped, different invisible kernels can yield the same image, so the image subgroup alone no longer records the entire acting group. This supports the page’s faithful qualification.')
add('AFF-MODULE','targeted proof check','Assume a regular abelian subgroup Gamma of Sym(E). A unital homomorphism k→End_Z(Gamma) gives Gamma a k-vector-space structure, and its regular action makes E an affine torsor under that vector space. Conversely the translation group of an affine k-space is regular and abelian and carries exactly that scalar endomorphism action. A prescribed dimension n additionally requires dim_k Gamma=n; the bare ring homomorphism does not specify dimension.')
add('PROJ-MODEL','targeted proof check','With projective k-structures defined by coordinate changes in PGL(n,k), transport along bijections from P^(n−1)(k) to a fixed set P gives the quotient Isom_ens(P^(n−1)(k),P)/PGL(n,k). Equality of the transported coordinate structures is precisely right precomposition by a projectivity. This verifies the model quotient as written, whose model has projective dimension n−1.')
add('PROJ-DIM','refuted','Under the standard projective dimension convention, the phrase dimension n does not match the model P^(n−1)(k) and group GP(n−1,k). For k=F2,n=2, the written model has three points while a projective plane of dimension two has seven. Refined statement id M014-PROJ-MODEL has dimension n−1; alternatively a dimension-n model requires P^n(k) and PGL(n+1,k). The text’s n and n−1 are preserved if confirmed by ink.')
add('HOMOGENIZATION','targeted proof check','The maps GL(n,k)→Aff(n,k)→PGL(n+1,k) are A↦(A,0) and (A,b)↦class([[A,b],[0,1]]). Block multiplication gives (A,b)(C,d)=(AC,Ad+b), so these are homomorphisms. The last map is injective because a scalar relation between two normalized matrices must have scalar one by their final entry. Geometrically the first map forgets the chosen affine origin; the second extends affine transformations to projective transformations of the dimension-n completion obtained from an (n+1)-dimensional homogeneous vector space.')
